-> T26 Phase 2: an object literal with a constrained (`∈`) field is a
-> Value::Schema — the set of objects satisfying those field domains — not
-> a resolved Object. `mm ∈ K` narrows mm structurally (open/at-least, not
-> exact); `mm = {...}` resolves it if the object satisfies K's fields.
K = { k ∈ String, m ∈ Number }
mm ∈ K
mm = { k = "dfdfdf", m = 3 }
assert mm.k = "dfdfdf"
assert mm.m = 3

-> T25's original mixed-field gap: a literal can mix `=` (exact) and `∈`
-> (constrained) fields — the whole literal becomes a schema.
a = { k = 2, L ∈ Z }
assert a ∈ Schema

-> Applying the same schema twice is idempotent (still open, not a
-> contradiction) and later resolves normally.
nn ∈ K
nn ∈ K
nn = { k = "a", m = 1 }
assert nn.m = 1
